export default "Right-sided lower abdominal pain at mid-cycle is usually mittelschmerz, ovulation pain from a follicle releasing on the right ovary. It can also be appendicitis, an ovarian cyst, or other pathology. Knowing the differences is important because one of those possibilities is a surgical emergency.\n\n## Mittelschmerz: What Normal Ovulation Pain Looks Like\n\nMittelschmerz occurs in a portion of people with regular ovulatory cycles. The mechanism: when the dominant follicle ruptures, it releases follicular fluid and sometimes a small amount of blood into the peritoneal cavity. This can irritate the peritoneum (the membrane lining the abdominal cavity), producing pain.\n\n**Characteristic features:**\n- **Location:** One side of the lower abdomen, whichever ovary released the egg that month. Typically switches sides from cycle to cycle (occasionally the same ovary releases two cycles in a row).\n- **Quality:** Dull ache, cramping, or a sharper twinge. Some people describe it as a pop.\n- **Duration:** Minutes to a few hours. Occasionally one to two days, but this is on the long end.\n- **Severity:** Mild to moderate. You can go about your day.\n- **Associated symptoms:** None beyond the pain itself. No fever, no nausea, no change in bowel habits, no loss of appetite.\n- **Cycle timing:** Mid-cycle, typically days 12 to 16 in a 28-day cycle (later in longer cycles).\n- **Concurrent signs:** Often occurs with or just before egg-white cervical mucus and LH surge.\n\nThe pain from follicle rupture typically peaks briefly then fades as the fluid disperses and the peritoneal irritation resolves.\n\n## Appendicitis: What Requires Immediate Attention\n\nAppendicitis is inflammation of the appendix, a small structure attached to the large intestine in the right lower quadrant. It can become life-threatening if it ruptures.\n\n**Characteristic features:**\n- **Location:** Right lower quadrant only. Never left-sided.\n- **Progression:** Worsens continuously over hours. Does not improve on its own.\n- **Starting location:** Pain often begins around the navel (periumbilical), then migrates to the right lower quadrant over 4 to 12 hours.\n- **Severity:** Progressively severe. Becomes difficult to move or walk as it progresses.\n- **Associated symptoms:** Nausea and vomiting (in most cases), loss of appetite, low-grade fever early (higher if perforated), sometimes diarrhea.\n- **Cycle timing:** Not related to the menstrual cycle. Can occur at any point in the cycle, in any month, at any age.\n- **Movement sensitivity:** Appendicitis pain typically worsens with movement, particularly jarring (bumps in a car, jumping).\n\n**Rebound tenderness:** If pressing firmly on the right lower abdomen and then quickly releasing causes a sharp spike in pain when you release (not when you press), this is rebound tenderness. It is a clinical sign of peritoneal inflammation consistent with appendicitis. This requires immediate emergency evaluation.\n\n## Side-by-Side Comparison\n\n| Feature | Mittelschmerz | Appendicitis |\n|---------|--------------|--------------|\n| Location | Right OR left | Right only |\n| Progression | Stable, improves | Worsening over hours |\n| Duration | Minutes to hours | Progresses until treated |\n| Severity | Mild-moderate | Progressive, severe |\n| Nausea/vomiting | No | Usually yes |\n| Fever | No | Usually yes |\n| Loss of appetite | No | Yes |\n| Cycle timing | Mid-cycle | No relation |\n| Rebound tenderness | No | Often yes |\n| Movement sensitivity | Mild at most | Often severe |\n\n## When It's Neither\n\nRight or left lower abdominal pain at mid-cycle that does not fit typical mittelschmerz may have other causes:\n\n**Ovarian cyst rupture:** A cyst on the ovary can rupture at ovulation, producing sudden, sharp, severe pain. Usually one-sided. If pain is sudden onset, severe, or accompanied by rapid heart rate or lightheadedness, seek emergency evaluation. A large cyst rupture can cause significant internal bleeding. Use the [ovarian cyst urgent-care notes](/free/ovarian-cyst-tracking-kit) after care is underway, not to decide whether to wait.\n\n**Hemorrhagic corpus luteum:** The corpus luteum (the structure left after ovulation) occasionally bleeds internally, causing sharp one-sided pain in the days after ovulation rather than at ovulation itself.\n\n**Endometriosis:** If ovulation pain is severe, lasting, and gets worse each cycle, this warrants investigation for endometriosis, particularly ovarian endometriomas (cysts on the ovary filled with old blood).\n\n**Ovarian torsion:** An ovary that twists on its ligaments produces sudden, severe, one-sided pain, often with nausea. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate evaluation.\n\n**Ectopic pregnancy:** One-sided lower abdominal pain in the early weeks after a potential conception, with a positive pregnancy test, requires immediate evaluation to rule out ectopic pregnancy.\n\n## Decision Guide\n\n**Seek emergency care immediately if:**\n- Right-sided pain worsening over hours\n- Any combination of severe pain plus fever plus vomiting\n- Sudden onset severe pain (either side), possible ovarian torsion or ruptured cyst\n- Pain with lightheadedness, rapid pulse, or fainting, possible internal bleeding\n- Rebound tenderness on examination\n\n**Monitor and follow up if:**\n- Mid-cycle, one-sided, brief, mild pain with no systemic symptoms, typical mittelschmerz\n- But add to your cycle tracking data and note if it worsens over cycles\n\n**Seek non-emergency evaluation if:**\n- Ovulation pain is severe (requiring stronger pain medication)\n- Duration exceeds two days\n- Pattern is worsening over cycles\n- Pain at mid-cycle is accompanied by deep pelvic pressure or pain with sex\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nLogging mid-cycle pain, timing, side, severity, and duration across two to three cycles reveals whether it is a consistent ovulation pattern or a changing one. Mittelschmerz is benign. Increasing severity or duration over cycles is a pattern that warrants investigation. Your tracking data provides the baseline that makes changes observable. If ovarian cyst pain is part of the concern, use the [ovarian cyst pain log](/free/ovarian-cyst-tracking-kit) to keep the notes short and visit-ready.";
